WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.097
(guitar strumming)

00:00:07.944 --> 00:00:10.234
- Playing Hawaiian
music, playing slack-key,

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:13.468
and the Paniolo way of life goes together,

00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:17.686
because after a hard
day's work on the ranch,

00:00:17.686 --> 00:00:19.312
you want to sit down and relax and listen

00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:21.312
to this beautiful music.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.802
My name is Leabert Lindsey.

00:00:23.802 --> 00:00:27.703
And I'm a fifth generation of
the Paniolo Hawaiian cowboy,

00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:30.703
and I play Hawaiian slack-key music.

00:00:32.855 --> 00:00:34.966
(twangy music)

00:00:34.966 --> 00:00:36.553
- [Chris] I'm Chris Funk.

00:00:36.553 --> 00:00:39.270
I'm a musician in a band
called The Decemberists,

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.126
and I'm on a journey looking

00:00:40.126 --> 00:00:42.486
for the most surprising and extraordinary

00:00:42.486 --> 00:00:43.742
people in music.

00:00:43.742 --> 00:00:47.075
My next stop takes me to Waimea, Hawaii.

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:50.014
There, I will meet up
with Leabert Lindsey,

00:00:50.014 --> 00:00:54.181
the unsung hero of Hawaii's
forgotten cowboy culture.

00:00:56.914 --> 00:00:58.874
(gentle music)

00:00:58.874 --> 00:01:01.346
Hawaii offers so much beauty,

00:01:01.346 --> 00:01:03.818
from cliffs rising out of the ocean

00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:07.459
to rolling hills and lush green valleys.

00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:09.050
But what surprised me when I got here

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.818
was the deep rooted community of cowboys,

00:01:11.818 --> 00:01:15.370
something normally associated
with the Southwest.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.194
- A lot of people, they don't realize

00:01:17.194 --> 00:01:19.434
how far back we go,

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:21.962
being Hawaiian cowboys.

00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:24.906
That's part of our heritage.

00:01:24.906 --> 00:01:27.655
We had cowboys here on the big island

00:01:27.655 --> 00:01:29.743
before there was any states west

00:01:29.743 --> 00:01:32.743
of the Mississippi, including Texas.

00:01:33.591 --> 00:01:35.788
- [Chris] The influence
of Spanish cowboys,

00:01:35.788 --> 00:01:39.808
called vaqueros, is ever
present in Hawaiian culture.

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:41.100
They came here in the 1800s

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.228
to teach the islanders to herd cattle,

00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:45.732
bringing with them a lasting way of life,

00:01:45.732 --> 00:01:48.456
culture, and a unique style of music,

00:01:48.456 --> 00:01:49.873
called slack-key.

00:01:51.388 --> 00:01:52.597
- Hey, how's it going?

00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:53.939
- [Chris] What's up?

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:54.772
Did I interrupt here?

00:01:54.772 --> 00:01:55.922
- [Leabert] You heard us from--

00:01:55.922 --> 00:01:57.388
- I just heard you from up there.

00:01:57.388 --> 00:02:00.520
- Okay well welcome welcome.
(laughing)

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.834
- [Leabert] Welcome.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:04.207
- [Chris] Slack-key style
involves loosening the string,

00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:06.427
while tuning to create
a sweet soothing sound

00:02:06.427 --> 00:02:09.966
synonymous with the
aloha nature of Hawaii.

00:02:09.966 --> 00:02:11.341
- [Leabert] When the vaqueros came here,

00:02:11.341 --> 00:02:12.777
they brought the guitar.

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:14.712
Along with them, they brought some

00:02:14.712 --> 00:02:16.626
open Spanish tunings.

00:02:16.626 --> 00:02:19.597
And they taught the Hawaiians
how to play these tunings.

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:22.006
So the Hawaiians played it, learned it,

00:02:22.006 --> 00:02:25.153
and then they created
their own style of playing,

00:02:25.153 --> 00:02:28.386
vaquero always called slack-key.

00:02:28.386 --> 00:02:29.219
(guitar music)

00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:31.780
♪ Paniolo ♪

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.280
♪ Oh paniolo ♪

00:02:35.171 --> 00:02:38.095
The cowboy does everything from A to Z,

00:02:38.095 --> 00:02:41.754
work the cattle, brand the cattle.

00:02:41.754 --> 00:02:44.319
Come on then, Paniolo.

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:48.268
And when the job’s done,
they play slack-key

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.569
and sometimes they play music

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:52.625
right there where they're at.

00:02:52.625 --> 00:02:56.332
I've never seen a Paniolo
without his guitar.

00:02:56.332 --> 00:02:58.915
It's like a fish without water.

00:02:59.882 --> 00:03:01.517
♪ Had a red banana ♪

00:03:01.517 --> 00:03:02.945
♪ Lauhala hat ♪

00:03:02.945 --> 00:03:04.601
♪ A bound gunny sack in his hand ♪

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.660
- The authenticity of
Leabert and the Paniolos

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.743
blew me away.

00:03:08.906 --> 00:03:11.201
Modern day folk music is
rooted in historical themes

00:03:11.201 --> 00:03:13.410
like coal mining and railroad work,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.537
even though today's musicians

00:03:14.537 --> 00:03:16.881
have never likely done these jobs.

00:03:16.881 --> 00:03:20.127
But Leabert embodies
everything about the lifestyle.

00:03:20.127 --> 00:03:21.837
He's a true Hawaiian cowboy,

00:03:21.837 --> 00:03:25.222
who also happens to be one of
the best slack-key guitarists.

00:03:25.222 --> 00:03:27.233
(guitar flourish)

00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:29.640
(laughing)

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:30.473
- Yes, sir.

00:03:32.252 --> 00:03:33.085
(gentle music)

00:03:33.085 --> 00:03:35.580
- That's a good one.
- Alright, yeah great.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.747
- I'm very appreciative to
the vaqueros who came here

00:03:41.364 --> 00:03:43.114
to teach us Hawaiians

00:03:44.347 --> 00:03:45.847
how to be cowboys.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.873
And we created our own style

00:03:52.502 --> 00:03:56.360
of being cowboys, which is a Paniolo.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.368
♪ Every time I looked at you ♪

00:03:59.368 --> 00:04:01.000
♪ I just don't know what to do ♪

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.804
It's something that is really valuable

00:04:03.804 --> 00:04:05.604
in our way of life.

00:04:05.604 --> 00:04:08.684
If you don't pass this knowledge down

00:04:08.684 --> 00:04:11.308
to the next generation,

00:04:11.308 --> 00:04:13.659
it's gonna be lost.

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:17.304
This is a really, really important part

00:04:17.304 --> 00:04:20.721
of our heritage that we want to continue.

00:04:21.717 --> 00:04:22.709
- If somebody's gonna call me Hawaiian,

00:04:22.709 --> 00:04:24.831
I'm not gonna say no.

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:25.828
- Hey, you're a Hawaiian.

00:04:25.828 --> 00:04:27.400
- Alright, good, yes, woo.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.595
(laughing)

00:04:29.595 --> 00:04:32.012
- [Leabert] You're a Paniolo.

